         Common Questions Am I Registered To Vote? If you are unsure of your voter status please click the following link:  Secretary of Massachusetts website . If you  are not  registered to vote, you can register using one of the methods listed below: Online Voter Registration System (via  Secretary of Massachusetts website ) In Person at the City Clerk's Office Paper Application (returned to City Clerk's Office via USPS mail, drop box at 50 Payson Ave, or hand delivery to the clerk's office.) Voter Registration Form (English) Voter Registration Form (Spanish) Where Do I Vote? All registered voters  in Easthampton vote at Easthampton High School located at  70 Williston Avenue. What Precinct Am I In? Easthampton has 5 precincts.   View the map (pdf)  or access the state's  interactive online map . The interactive map defaults to an area near Boston, but enter your address in the search field to get local data.  How Do I Apply to Vote-By-Mail? The following methods may be used to apply to vote-by-mail Online Mail-In Ballot Application System ( via Secretary of Massachusetts website ) Complete the application form and return it to our office by mail, drop box at 50 Payson Ave, or hand-delivery. Vote-By-Mail Application Form (English) Vote-By-Mail Application Form (Spanish) Vote-By-Mail Information (State & Local) The VOTES Act (2022) expanded early voting by providing individuals the opportunity to vote early by mail with "no excuse" necessary. No-excuse early voting by mail is available for the following: State Elections State Primaries Presidential Primaries City of Easthampton Biennial Elections  *(please see note) The Secretary of State's office is required by law to send a pre-addressed, postage pre-paid Vote-By-Mail application to every registered vote prior to each  state election . *IMPORTANT NOTE: The local elections office is not required to send a vote-by-mail application to every registered voter in the community. We do not, by law, maintain a list of who has voted by mail in the past. If you wish to vote-by-mail for an upcoming local election you MUST apply every year. We cannot accept a verbal request in person or over the phone. The state requires we receive an application from each individual voter with a written (not typed) signature.  Vote-By Mail applications must be received by our office  no later than 5:00 p.m. on the 5th business day   before the election . It does not matter when the application was postmarked. We must physically have the application by the deadline. NOTE:  All vote-by-mail requests for the November 3, 2026 State Election have been fulfilled.  Ballots continue to be mailed daily as applications are received. 
     

 
